#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097478} :activity/id #uuid "fe9cb489-c107-59bc-888d-07dcb3a33049" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715 :topic/id #uuid "0d3ad89a-14be-5528-a12e-4252d281243e" :topic/math-academy-id 1103} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097832 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2\\mathbf{a} &= 2 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1.5\\mathbf{a} &= 1.5 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a$, and are parallel to each other." :tutorial/id #uuid "977eec28-841a-556b-a6c4-937a625db1b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bd9397e1-06d5-59ff-a830-f9894033fdaf" :step/math-academy-id 15697 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096445 :question/id #uuid "069b2882-f4a9-589f-a903-4e89d077cd85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4279"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d5c96d-ca13-5c1e-83d3-0dc9f6da0f83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504848} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504841}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"} :step/id #uuid "993ef18e-6262-5636-9d9a-4e0c91fc4973" :step/math-academy-id 15698 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :question/id #uuid "d1329498-4413-5d87-a814-be0d57a3afd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4280"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc49f831-8e1d-5fb4-8952-1dd4ee3bfa02" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504023} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504021}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "f0e71ce6-22dd-5638-81d3-c222aada5c61" :step/math-academy-id 15699 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097834 :tutorial/content "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector:\n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5934891-d63d-5d05-be3e-b864a8081187" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2907 :tutorial/title "The Zero Vector is Parallel to Any Vector"} :step/id #uuid "a19c5fdc-f46e-5a71-be1f-0f7a02107fb3" :step/math-academy-id 15700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097852 :tutorial/content "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation\n$\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}$\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "9fc7b4c2-c418-5980-88be-ea7609a45ba0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3401 :tutorial/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors Are Parallel"} :step/id #uuid "afa24342-fd53-5361-8f24-8c57d1a13102" :step/math-academy-id 19014 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096494 :question/id #uuid "11c09a26-0e83-5a77-b777-aea8a3594960" :question/math-academy-id "e-5183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505000} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504932} {:db/id #edb/ref 175